        "band_question": {
          "type": "narrative",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The question I couldn't put down",
            "body": "<p>My son's skin reacted to ordinary high-street products when he was a baby, the kind most people never think twice about. I'm a chemist and biochemist by training, so rather than switching brands I started reading the labels. One question wouldn't leave me alone. How much of what goes into a typical skincare product is doing something for the skin, and how much is just along for the ride?</p><p>That is a dangerous question to put to a chemist. You don't stop at \"I wonder\". You start reading the research.</p><p>My own skin has never been the straightforward kind either. I've lived with rosacea, adult acne, a sensitivity to wool, and dermatographia, which brings me up in welts at the lightest scratch. Later, in middle age, came Type 2 diabetes after a bout of pancreatitis, and Raynaud's. Both affect circulation, and both made me take looking after my skin more seriously still. A moisturiser is not an answer to any of those, and I would never pretend otherwise. What living with all that teaches you is that good skincare is mostly about comfort, and that the gentle, unshowy version is usually the one that gets you through the day.</p>",
            "links": ""
          }
        },
        "band_literature": {
          "type": "narrative",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Six years in the literature",
            "body": "<p>Label-reading turned into a standing habit and rolling eyes in the family. I spent well over six years reading the research on the human microbiome, the community of microbes that lives on us and in us. I started with the gut and worked my way to the skin, the more overlooked of the two.</p><p>The skin microbiome matters to skin health, and it is still being mapped. On the evidence so far, the best way to support it is gently: a product that is slightly acidic (pH 5 to 5.5), close to the pH of healthy skin, with nothing in it that strips, rather than the miracle compound of the month. Biotaderm is built on that.</p>",
            "links": "<p><a href=\"/pages/the-skin-microbiome\">The skin microbiome</a></p>"
          }
        },
        "band_whatimade": {
          "type": "narrative",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What I made",
            "body": "<p>Fifteen moisturisers, which sounds like a lot until you see the shape of it: three textures across five skin types. Flow is the lightest, for the face. Core is the most versatile, for anywhere on the body. Deep is the richest, for hands, elbows and feet. The difference between them is how much cold-pressed and organic plant oil each one carries.</p><p>Three principles determine the ingredients.</p><p>Nature, which means natural, organic and naturally derived ingredients. We only use synthetic ingredients when they are the same as their natural counterparts and it is more environmentally sensible to do so.</p><p>Science, which means ingredients chosen on published research. I read the published research on an ingredient before choosing it.</p><p>Ethics, which means recyclable aluminium bottles, a pump you keep, and B Corp certification I am working towards slowly and properly. Everything is vegan, and nothing is tested on animals, other than me.</p>",
            "links": "",
